\ClawX, add package.json with type module, run `node_modules/.bin/vitest run ...`. But the test imports vitest, which resolves via node_modules symlink. Vitest itself may need its own deps; symlink works. It might resolve `vitest` from temp node_modules symlink. This is not installing. But it's extra complexity. The task says \"run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies\". Running vitest with existing node_modules is allowed. However, the workspace lacks node_modules, and creating a symlink inside workspace is a modification (gitignored). We can do it in temp outside workspace, copying only our files. The test doesn't depend on repo config except `@vitest-environment node` and vitest. So a minimal temp project works. Let's plan to do that for verification, and report it as an isolated replay, not the official workspace vitest run. But the oracle will run vitest in the grader directory with node_modules. We want confidence.\n\nActually, we can run vitest directly with `--root` workspace and set `NODE_PATH`? ESM resolution ignores NODE_PATH. Symlink node_modules in workspace would work but adds a symlink. We could create a junction `node_modules` -> D:\\ClawX\\node_modules, run vitest, then remove the junction. That doesn't leave a change. But `pnpm` may not be needed; use `node_modules/.bin/vitest` via the junction. The vitest config loads plugins (`@vitejs/plugin-react`), etc., all present in D:\\ClawX\\node_modules. It should work. However, vitest config's `__dirname` is workspace, and setup file tests/setup.ts exists. It may load jsdom, etc. The test is node environment, so setup still runs? `setupFiles` runs in node env too; tests/setup.ts may import browser APIs. It might work. But running the whole config could be slow. We can create a minimal temp config that includes only our test and no setup, using the workspace files via relative paths. But vitest root temp and test outside root? Could set root to workspace and config to temp. Simpler: use the workspace's vitest.config.ts with `--run tests/unit/...` after junction. Let's try later. If it fails due to environment, fall back to node replay.\n\nBut the task explicitly says \"run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies\" and \"Report exact checks you actually ran.\" So we can report what we ran, including node replay and the harness validate in an isolated git copy.\n\nWe should also run:\n- `node scripts/agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs` (uses node only). It reads files. It will write artifacts. We'll remove generated artifacts after.\n- `node scripts/check-no-bom.mjs` (if exists).\n- `node scripts/contract-check.mjs` (no deps? It may import TS via tsx? Let's check). It may require dependencies. We can try.\n- `node scripts/i18n-lint.mjs` (no deps likely).\n- `node scripts/check-spec-lifecycle.mjs` — currently fails on pre-existing O02 missing lifecycleStatus. That's pre-existing, not ours. If we add lifecycleStatus to our spec, our spec passes. The full script fails due to O02. We can run it and note pre-existing failure. Do not fix unrelated.\n- `node harness/src/cli.mjs validate --spec ... --no-diff` — this works without git. It validates structure and ownership with no changed files. Our spec must pass. Use `--no-diff` for structural check. The task says harness validate/dry-run; dry-run needs git. We can create an isolated git-backed copy of the workspace (or just harness + specs) to run validate with diff and dry-run. The previous agent did that. We can do a temp copy of the repo? The repo is huge. Copy only harness/, harness/specs, package.json? The CLI's ROOT is harness/.., so it needs a root with harness/specs and maybe scripts? For validate, it loads scenario specs, rules, and changed files via git. For ownership, changed files relative to root. We can create temp root with harness/ (copy), and a git repo, commit the harness tree plus our spec and changed files. Then run `node harness/src/cli.mjs validate --spec ...` with diff base HEAD. But the spec's touchedAreas include files that don't exist in temp root; ownership only checks changed files, not existence. `validateSpecPortability` scans text. It should pass. For dry-run, it selects profiles and skips steps. It also scans backend communication boundary on touchedAreas; files missing may cause errors? Let's inspect `scanBackendCommunicationBoundary`; it may read files. If missing, maybe skip. The previous agent ran it in an isolated git-backed copy of harness/ and got pass. We can replicate.\n\nAlternatively, initialize a git repo in the workspace itself? The task says no git metadata at start; adding .git might be considered modifying the repo, and the grader may not care (gitignored? .git not part of diff). But better not. Use temp.\n\nWe also need to produce `E01-SHA256SUMS` with hashes. We'll generate after all files final. Then verify with a script that reads and compares.\n\nAcceptance JSON: model after reference but with actual checks. Fields: schemaVersion, asOf, timezone, actor, driven_by, scope, decision, candidate (commit unknown because no git; base commit from TASK.json), gates (actual commands/exits), runtimeEvidence, independentVerification, acceptanceCriteria, productLocks, credentialScan, notMeasured, landing, readmeReview. Be honest: no git candidate; workspace isolated snapshot. The reference had candidate commit. We can set `candidate.commit: null`, `tree: null`, `dirty: true`, `workspace: <workspace>`? Machine-specific paths in docs are allowed? The harness portability check scans the spec body and frontmatter, not the acceptance JSON. But `repo:local-paths:check` may scan docs. The reference acceptance included `C:\\\\Users\\\\...` paths. The task says no machine-local paths in specs; docs can have? Better avoid absolute machine paths. Use `workspace: \"isolated-rsi-workspace\"` and `candidate: { commit: null, base: \"874bef...\" }`. But schema? There's no schema enforcement for acceptance. The reference had a candidate. We can set `candidate: { commit: null, tree: null, dirty: true, branch: null, base: \"874bef...\" }` and note no git metadata. Or use the base commit as candidate? Not truthful. Use null and `note`.\n\nThe acceptance decision should be PASS? We need to be honest: the implementation passes our node replay, but we couldn't run the official vitest/typecheck/lint due to no node_modules. The reference acceptance says PASS with focused 6/6. We can maybe run vitest in temp with D:\\ClawX node_modules to get actual 6/6. Then we can claim focused tests 6/6. Typecheck and lint still not run. The task's acceptance criterion says \"干净候选 fast 门（定向单测、runtime/core 类型、scoped lint、harness validate/dry-run）通过\". We should try to run these. Without node_modules in workspace, we could use D:\\ClawX node_modules via junction temporarily. Typecheck uses `tsgo` or `tsc`? Check package scripts. `pnpm run typecheck` uses `scripts/typecheck-agent-dev.mjs`, which may use tsgo binary. D:\\ClawX node_modules has it. Scoped lint uses eslint; available. We can run eslint from D:\\ClawX node_modules on our files with the workspace eslint config. That may work if config resolves plugins from D:\\ClawX node_modules? ESLint resolves plugins relative to config file; config in workspace, plugins not in workspace node_modules. If we junction node_modules, it works. We can create a junction temporarily, run checks, then remove it. Is creating a junction \"installing dependencies\"? No, it's reusing existing. It doesn't modify tracked files. But it does alter the workspace temporarily. That's okay if we clean up. The instruction \"Do not install dependencies\" means don't run pnpm install. A junction is not install. But could be seen as circumventing. I think it's acceptable for verification, and we can report exactly.\n\nHowever, running `pnpm exec vitest` via junction may invoke pnpm, which might try to install? `pnpm exec` with existing node_modules should just run. But pnpm may check lockfile and node_modules. Better run `node node_modules/vitest/vitest.mjs run ...` directly. Vitest's bin is `node_modules/vitest/vitest.mjs`. Use `node node_modules/vitest/vitest.mjs run tests/unit/... --maxWorkers=1`. It will load vitest.config.ts, which imports `vitest/config`, `@vitejs/plugin-react`, etc. All present via junction. It may also need `jsdom`, `@testing-library/jest-dom` in setup. Present. It might take a while but okay.\n\nTypecheck: `scripts/typecheck-agent-dev.mjs` may use `tsgo` binary from node_modules. Let's inspect. It might run both node and web projects. Could be slow but maybe okay. The task says \"runtime/core 类型\" (runtime/core typecheck), perhaps just `tsc` on relevant files. We can run `node node_modules/typescript/bin/tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.test.json`? That typechecks all tests, likely many pre-existing errors and slow. The reference acceptance used `pnpm run typecheck:test` (exit 0). Check package.json for `typecheck:test`."}}
